<h2>A conflict written into the contract</h2>
<p>DDA&rsquo;s tenders for prefabricated RCC housing split the work into two groups, awarded in April 2013. Clause 9.1.2 tied minimum cement content to the CPWD Specifications 2009 and IS:456 — 330 kg/cum, with a maximum of 500. Clause 9.1.3 fixed 360 kg/cum for &ldquo;any grade RCC&rdquo; and set recovery thresholds of 410 kg (M-25) and 428 kg (M-35) — but none for M-30. The contractor flagged the conflict at both pre-bid meetings; DDA answered that the NIT provision would prevail. When DDA directed design mixes above 330 kg/cum in execution, the contractor claimed the extra cement cost, arbitration followed in 2015, and the tribunal — applying contra proferentem — treated 330 kg/cum as the baseline for all grades, awarding Rs. 3,16,78,614 for excess M-30 cement with a Rs. 6/kg rate going forward.</p>

<h2>Where the award crossed the Section 34 line</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interpretation is the tribunal&rsquo;s domain.</strong> The Court repeated that it <em>&ldquo;does not exercise appellate jurisdiction over an arbitral award&rdquo;</em>, and left the M-25 and M-35 findings intact as plausible readings of clauses 9.1.2 and 9.1.3.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>But an express term cannot be overridden.</strong> For M-30, the tribunal had, in the Court&rsquo;s words, overridden <em>&ldquo;the specific and unambiguous requirement of 360 kg per cubic metre expressly stipulated under Clause 9.1.3.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No recovery threshold &ne; a right to payment.</strong> The absence of a right to recover from the contractor does not create a reciprocal right to be paid; the claim was never pleaded as quantum meruit, and no contractual provision supported it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Equity needs authorisation.</strong> An award resting on fairness or equity is impermissible without the parties&rsquo; express authorisation under Section 28(2); an award that <em>&ldquo;ignores express contractual stipulations or re-writes the contract suffers from patent illegality under Section 34.&rdquo;</em></div>
</div>

<h2>The operative outcome</h2>
<p>Both awards were set aside to the extent they: treated 330 kg/cum as the M-30 minimum; awarded Rs. 3,16,78,614 for excess M-30 cement up to 30 June 2015; directed payment at Rs. 6/kg thereafter; directed refund of M-30 recoveries resting on the same reciprocal-entitlement logic; and awarded consequential interest. Both petitions were allowed in those terms, the remaining findings — including on jurisdiction and the other grades — left undisturbed, with no order as to costs. The Court applied SEAMEC Ltd. v. Oil India Ltd., (2020) 5 SCC 164, on the limits of permissible construction.</p>

<h2>Drafting and arbitration lessons</h2>
<p>The dispute is a monument to an unresolved pre-bid query: both sides saw the clash between 330 and 360 kg/cum before contract, and a one-line clarification would have prevented a decade of litigation. For counsel resisting awards, the case illustrates the most reliable Section 34 ground in current practice — not re-arguing interpretation, but isolating the point where the tribunal stopped construing the contract and started supplementing it. And for claimants, it is a reminder that a claim without a clause must be pleaded for what it is — quantum meruit or damages for breach — because a tribunal cannot be left to source the entitlement in fairness after the fact.</p>
<div class="note">Section 34 findings do not re-decide the commercial merits; they test the award against the grounds in the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996. Case details are as recorded in the judgment dated 23 November 2026.</div>
